{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgr4zVwkj7cAF51GVXkRa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018dDLESx1VzmKthNEyqSi6H","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"candidates":[{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.confirmEmail","start_line":300,"end_line":340,"explanation":"I could not see this method in the snippets, so the line range is an estimate and the diagnosis is inferred. The user's provider is 'email' before confirmation, because login worked then. It only fails after the confirm step. The confirm step probably writes a wrong provider value, for example 'google' or a leftover social provider, when it activates the user. Login then hits the `user.provider !== AuthProvidersEnum.email` check in validateLogin and returns needLoginViaProvider:google."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.validateSocialLogin","start_line":136,"end_line":143,"explanation":"If an inactive user with a matching email exists, this block overwrites their provider and socialId with the social provider's values. It does this without checking that the email account is the one being linked, and the inactive/unconfirmed state is the one a freshly registered email user has. This is an unusual account takeover and provider overwrite, and it would produce needLoginViaProvider:google. It only runs when a Google login is attempted, so it fits the report less well than a confirm-time bug."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.validateLogin","start_line":51,"end_line":55,"explanation":"This is where the 422 is raised. The check relies on the stored user.provider being correct. It is a symptom site, not the root cause, and the stored provider value is what needs fixing."}],"fix":{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"In confirmEmail, only set the user's status to active and leave provider unchanged (it must stay 'email'). Do not set a social provider there